{{!
	Notion skin: the link primitive.

	Renders one anchor of skin chrome - a main-menu entry, a namespace or action tab, a
	dropdown row, a footer place, a user-menu item. It is the plain half of a menu item:
	MenuListItem.mustache renders Button.mustache from the positive data-button section
	and this partial from the inverted one, so a record carrying button styling never
	arrives here. The only partial it includes in turn is Icon.mustache.

	@typedef object attribute
	@prop string key the attribute name, for example href, class, title, accesskey, rel,
		id, or any aria- or data- name core chose to emit for this link
	@prop string value the attribute value, which arrives UNESCAPED from core and is
		therefore escaped here with a double-brace expression

	attribute[] array-attributes every attribute of the anchor, expanded one record at a
		time. Both href and class arrive this way, which is exactly why neither is written
		literally below: core assembled the class list before the data reached the skin,
		and a repeated class attribute would be discarded by the HTML parser rather than
		merged. An empty list is normal and yields a bare anchor.
	string|null icon optional Codex icon name for the leading glyph. A falsy value renders
		nothing at all - that is how a label-only link is expressed - and a truthy one is
		handed straight to Icon.mustache, which reads the name as the current context and
		so needs no key of its own.
	string text the link label, which also arrives unescaped from core and is escaped
		below.

	Where this data comes from, which is worth reading before renaming any key. These are
	core's own portlet link records, built by
	MediaWiki\Skin\Components\SkinComponentLink::makeLink() and delivered to the skin as
	data-portlets.*.array-items[].array-links[]. NotionComponentMenu passes each record
	through untouched apart from overriding icon and, where a menu asks for button
	styling, swapping the whole record for a data-button one; it never rewrites a record
	into the shape NotionComponentLink returns. That class emits a pre-serialised
	html-attributes string alongside a separate href and has no call site in the rendering
	path - it exists for parity with Vector and for unit-test coverage - so interpolating
	html-attributes here would render nothing on a real page while silently dropping the
	href. This partial is deliberately written against the core record shape, exactly as
	mediawiki-skins-Vector/includes/templates/Link.mustache is.

	DO NOT REMOVE data-mw-interface, and do not make it conditional. Core's own client
	modules test for its PRESENCE, not its value, to decide that a link is trusted skin
	chrome rather than user-generated content: mediawiki.page.watch.ajax matches
	.mw-watchlink a[data-mw-interface] to upgrade the watchstar into an AJAX toggle,
	mediawiki.misc-authed-curate matches the patrol and rollback links the same way, and
	mediawiki.page.ready's selectorLogoutLink matches #pt-logout a[data-mw-interface].
	Every anchor this partial renders is chrome, so the attribute is emitted
	unconditionally and without a value; where core additionally supplies it through
	array-attributes - it does for the logout and watch links - the repetition is inert,
	because presence is all any of those selectors tests.

	Appearance is owned entirely by the stylesheet. This partial writes no class of its
	own, not even a notion- one, and no inline style: anchors are styled by
	resources/skins.notion.styles/links.less, which applies the Codex link mixins through
	the deliberately low-specificity selector a:where( :not( [role='button'] ) ) so that
	gadgets, user styles and extensions can still override them. The gap between glyph and
	label belongs to components/Icon.less, and that is why the four tags below are joined
	by empty comments: a real newline between them would collapse to a text space inside
	this inline element and add whitespace no stylesheet asked for.

	For the same reason the closing delimiter of this comment is glued to the anchor rather
	than left on a line of its own. Please do not tidy that up. A comment is only stripped
	together with its line when it stands alone on that line of OUTPUT, and this partial is
	rendered mid-line - MenuListItem.mustache invokes it inside a list item - so a newline
	between the delimiter and the anchor is not standalone and survives into the page as a
	stray text node the reference skin does not emit.
}}<a data-mw-interface {{#array-attributes}} {{key}}="{{value}}"{{/array-attributes}}>{{!
	}}{{#icon}}{{>Icon}}{{/icon}}{{!
	}}<span>{{text}}</span>{{!
}}</a>
